itions"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"b-is-even","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Under the temporary assumption, b is even.","step_ids":["substitute-a","b-square-even","b-even"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived in step lowest-terms-conflict from the least-denominator property and the evenness of a and b.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["lowest-terms-representation","a-is-even","b-is-even"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"lowest-terms-contradiction","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The temporary rationality assumption yields a contradiction.","step_ids":["lowest-terms-conflict"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Reductio in step conclude-irrational, after discharge of the local rationality assumption at lowest-terms-conflict.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["lowest-terms-contradiction"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"irrationality-conclusion","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"sqrt(2) is irrational.","step_ids":["lowest-terms-conflict","conclude-irrational"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"parity-decomposition","justification":"By the division algorithm with divisor 2 (proved from the well-ordering principle), n=2q+r for integers q,r with r equal to 0 or 1. Thus n=2q or n=2q+1.","statement":"Establish the parity dichotomy for every integer n."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"rational-representation","justification":"By the temporary rationality assumption and the definition of rationality, choose integers m,n with n nonzero and sqrt(2)=m/n.","statement":"Choose an integer representation sqrt(2)=m/n with n nonzero."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["rational-representation"],"id":"positive-denominator","justification":"If n is negative, replace m,n by -m,-n; otherwise retain them. This gives a representation with positive denominator.","statement":"Obtain a representation of sqrt(2) having a positive integer denominator."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["positive-denominator"],"id":"least-denominator","justification":"The set of positive denominators occurring in integer representations of sqrt(2) is nonempty by step positive-denominator. Apply the well-ordering principle and call its least element b; choose its associated numerator a.","statement":"Choose integers a,b with b the least positive denominator among all integer representations of sqrt(2)."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["least-denominator"],"id":"lowest-terms-property","justification":"If an integer g>1 divided both a and b, write a=gc and b=gd. Since b is positive, d is positive and d<b; moreover sqrt(2)=c/d. This contradicts the minimality of b. Hence no such g exists.","statement":"The selected integers a,b have no common divisor greater than 1."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["least-denominator"],"id":"square-equation","justification":"Square sqrt(2)=a/b, use (sqrt(2))^2=2, and multiply by the nonzero number b^2.","statement":"Derive a^2=2b^2."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["square-equation"],"id":"a-square-even","justification":"The equation a^2=2b^2 writes a^2 as twice an integer.","statement":"a^2 is even."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["a-square-even","parity-decomposition"],"id":"a-even","justification":"By parity decomposition, a is even or a=2k+1. In the latter case a^2=4k^2+4k+1=2(2k^2+2k)+1, which is not even, contradicting step a-square-even.","statement":"There is an integer c such that a=2c."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["square-equation","a-even"],"id":"substitute-a","justification":"Substitute a=2c into a^2=2b^2 to get 4c^2=2b^2, then divide by 2.","statement":"Derive b^2=2c^2."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["substitute-a"],"id":"b-square-even","justification":"The equation b^2=2c^2 writes b^2 as twice an integer.","statement":"b^2 is even."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["b-square-even","parity-decomposition"],"id":"b-even","justification":"By parity decomposition, b is even or b=2k+1. The latter makes b^2=2(2k^2+2k)+1, contradicting step b-square-even.","statement":"There is an integer d such that b=2d."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["lowest-terms-property","a-even","b-even"],"id":"lowest-terms-conflict","justification":"Steps a-even and b-even show that 2 divides both a and b, contrary to step lowest-terms-property. This contradiction discharges the temporary rationality assumption.","statement":"Obtain a contradiction."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["lowest-terms-conflict"],"id":"conclude-irrational","justification":"The assumption that sqrt(2) is rational has been discharged by contradiction; therefore sqrt(2) is not rational.","statement":"Conclude that sqrt(2) is irrational."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}
